[Music]  it has become a tradition here on the  bioneer to post a new batman workout at  the start of each new year  the idea is to tie together many of the  concepts i've discussed throughout the  year  with a program that will develop real  functional cross-modal performance  batman is the ideal fictional  inspiration for the kind of training i  covet  here's the imaginary peak of human  performance he is at once strong  fast agile and extremely intelligent  this year i wanted to do things a little  bit differently the aim is to approach  batman as though he were a client  looking for a functional training  program  rather than a general program to build a  number of different traits like batman  i'm going to instead imagine that bruce  wayne is a real person  with the specific set of skills that he  needs to develop and maintain  all with an extremely busy schedule  what's really interesting to me about  batman is that he shows how multiple  skills and tools are better than just  one  it seems to me that a lot of people are  low key training to be better fighters  these are not competitive boxers but  regular folk who still feel an  underlying pressure to be tough  in quotation marks to many people the  reason to lift heavy weights is so that  they could use that strength in a fight  of course this is missing the point that  technique is more important than raw  strength  when it comes to punching power and more  to the point fighting is not the only  expression of strength and fitness  i much prefer george's herbert's notion  of being  strong to be useful i get more  confidence knowing that if i need to run  away from danger i could do so quickly  and without tiring  that i could climb over obstacles or  lift another person over one shoulder  and help them get to safety  these same skills help you to get places  quickly help people move furniture and  focus on work  which is why batman training is  something that everyone should concern  themselves with  batman and in fact any action hero  really epitomized this idea  when you see batman take on a gang of  thugs or chase catwoman across rooftops  what are just as impressive as his  fighting prowess are his parkour  proprioception stealth strategy reflexes  quick decision making and cardio the  biggest challenge  most people would face when trying to be  batman for real would be getting tired  out almost  instantly or getting knocked out from a  single punch or jumping from a height  and twisting an ankle  or being really cold on top of a  building and getting a headache  this is why lifting heavy weights won't  make you batman and focusing on being  tough  is often just an ego trip trying to be  batman  obviously isn't an eager trip functional  coaches tend to choose exercises based  on specificity  these are exercises that either resemble  the movement the individual is training  for  or that utilize and strengthen specific  muscle groups and underlying traits  used in those actions this means the  exercises should have  near transfer if you want to get better  at chopping wood  it's no good to perform bench press and  squats instead you need something that  is similarly ballistic  that is simultaneously a pull and a push  and that incorporates a twisting element  the therapy effect  such exercises will train the body in  the transverse plane  meaning they incorporate twisting and  this will build stronger  more explosive serratus muscles so what  are the skills and traits that batman  training should address  work capacity batman needs to maintain  high power output for a long duration  strength endurance is as important if  not more important than maximum strength  cardio batman runs a lot while wearing a  lot of armor  running jumping and climbing batman  needs parkour skills  batman's strength must be relative to  his weight throwing a name  batman needs to train his throwing arm  for hurling batarangs he also needs  precise aim and so likely should train  his senses  max strength whether lifting a heavy  barrel grappling an opponent or lifting  open a door batman definitely needs some  max strength  what's important is that his strength is  developed on all planes  rotation is as important if not more  important than strength in the sagittal  and frontal planes  harden the bones to reduce injury  psychomotor vigilance  batman needs to be sharp and focused  even after a long night of chasing bad  guys  and equilibrioception or balance batman  needs body awareness  when balancing along beams or fighting  multiple combatants  explosiveness as it relates to jumping  and throwing punches  neck strength this may seem oddly  specific but it's important to reduce  concussions  and can even aid with visual tracking  thanks to the link between the neck and  the senses  grip strength whether climbing or  holding onto an assailant grip strength  is crucial  stability batman needs stability in the  hips knees and core to prevent injury  and enhances grappling and running rapid  decision making  mental toughness in spades there are  many more traits and skills that batman  can benefit from too but which should be  considered less important  things like mobility batman doesn't need  to be able to get his leg behind his  head but a certain amount of mobility  will aid in combat climbing and injury  prevention  i also strongly believe that batman  could benefit from being ambidextrous  with all that in mind let's now select  some exercises for batman  based on the specificity principle  sled push or pull rope climbing medicine  ball rotational throw or sledgehammer  medicine ball slam kettlebell swings  muscle ups  gamma cast atlas swings rock climbing  tire flipping  sandbags snatch band pull and push body  weight rows  military press lizard crawls skipping  loaded carries  isometric neck pushes  ab roll out bent rows hill sprints 100  up  rocking weighted squat jumps high rep  push-ups pull-ups squats  v-ups and burpees and heavy squat bench  press and deadlift  lateral jumps these are skater hops  renegade rows long distance running  trail running  heavy bag training and battle ropes this  is by no means a comprehensive list  but it's certainly a good starting point  so now how would we possibly go about  programming something like this  this is a criticism that some people  have made of my training style in  general  that it's impossible to train such a  variety of different traits and skills  if you have a busy schedule  people accuse me of having no life me  the guy talking about batman on youtube  well firstly no one has a life right now  so i don't know what you're so smug  about  secondly i still have plenty of time for  do maternal and my family come to think  of it  in all seriousness i only train for  around four to six hours a week and i  take whole weeks off regularly  nobody would accuse me of not having any  other interests or never taking it easy  i run several businesses and i'm a  full-time dad this list of exercises is  not that much more than you would expect  to train if you're using a bodybuilding  bro split  or calisthenics regime as for sequencing  the key is to perform heavy and complex  movements at the start  with lower reps then move to higher rep  low weight exercise  and then eventually finish with cardio  and work capacity training  this is similar in structure to a power  building routine  this way you can train every type of  twitch muscle fiber train all  energy systems train plyometric strength  as well as isometric strength  and avoid injury we're not doing heavy  compound lifts for high reps a lacrosse  fit staying away from heavy dangerous  movements especially when fatigued  is good advice for active batman  training in general  not only do we want to avoid injury  obviously we also don't want to  completely fatigue our nervous system  and therefore have no energy left for  the actual  job of fighting crime which i take it  we're all gonna do after this  this is something that a lot of people  don't recognize when writing training  programs  for specific skills sports or tasks the  aim isn't always to lift as much as  humanly possible  or to completely tax the individual some  people have commented about bruce lee's  gym cards that show he squatted 95  pounds  for 3x10 reps they take this as evidence  that he was  weak but bruce lee was a martial artist  first and foremost  it wouldn't make any sense for him to  train to the point of complete failure  and to thus be unable to strike the bag  the following day  moreover bruce lee would use  compensatory acceleration to increase  his rate of force development and neural  drive  even with a lighter weight it's all  about intent rather than the weight on  the bar  emotional [ __ ] this is why bar speed is  such a useful measure  if anything this shows that bruce lee  understood the principles of athletic  training  well before his time and the same should  definitely be true for bruce wayne  you might expect a batman training  program to involve lots of  hugely ridiculous lifts but in reality  it should be  far from that in fact if i were really  writing a training program for bruce  wayne  i would write it so that he never  performed his one rep max while he was  on active duty  if this were a periodized program we  might include that to get him to a  certain level of strength  and then simply aim to keep him there  this is much easier to do and would  allow him to continue delivering his  best effort  when punching the joker nor do all these  exercises need to be present in every  workout we can mix and match  performing high rep finishes and  resistance cardio work at the end of a  workout  also allows us to push ourselves and dig  deep to find reserves of strength and  determination  batman's willpower is his real  superpower and you can train that just  like a muscle  don't aim to make training comfortable  aim to make it cold  wet and grueling moreover it puts us in  a fatigued state and highly  physiologically aroused  which is where we can train for more  psychomotor vigilance and more  focus here's the thing batman needs to  be able to perform under extreme fatigue  and pressure  he needs to stay calm when he's just  been in a 10 minute fight this is what  makes the difference between saving the  day  and being a useless wreck and this is  what we see when we look at special  forces training  this is the kind of thing as well that  pat mcnamara talks about a lot  pat is an ex-spec ops fitness guy who  trains people by getting them to run  long distances  and then make precise shots with  firearms this kind of training is truly  valuable if you want to be a real action  hero  because it's a million miles from doing  the same thing whilst you're completely  rested in a safe environment  while batman doesn't use guns the same  thing applies to any other task that  requires attention  and a steady hand such as disarming a  bomb  this would be a perfect opportunity then  to practice ambidexterity or target  practice or both simultaneously  and it's at this point that our training  goes beyond being a tough  cross modal workout and turns into  something that batman might actually  find useful  and as jc santana from the institute of  human performance explained to me  losing concentration for even a second  during a fight is a game over  without psychomotor vigilance you'll  find that your movements start to become  less fast and explosive  there's a difference between grinding  and staying sharp and some of this comes  down to self-management and tactics  training cannot become rote or  absent-minded if we really want to work  out like batman  again this has huge benefits outside of  batman training too  psycho motor vigilance is a huge game  changer when it comes to injury  prevention  and accident avoidance it could  certainly prevent a lot of traffic  accidents  batman is required to operate in an  extremely unpredictable and changing  environment then  he might be running while carrying a  bomb one minute then dodging lasers  whilst injured the next to be effective  his training needs to reflect that  unpredictability  this is something i've talked about a  fair bit this year to develop skills  that are truly transferable  we can't always train on flat surfaces  with round bars  better than a pull-up is a pull-up on a  tree branch better than yet another  squat is a squat on a sloped surface  note that this only becomes true once  you have mastered the fundamental  movement  through repetition again a true batman  workout would need periodization to  account for all of this  think about muscles not only in terms of  force production but in terms of input  and output  that's to say that your muscles provide  crucial feedback about the world around  you  the forces acting against you and your  current position and must then  organize themselves around this movement  to generate useful force in the moment  you can't separate force production from  the visual motor landscape  without falling on your butt this is why  we need to learn to produce power  in unexpected ways if we want to be  truly functional and this is why  something like a sandbag snatch is so  useful  because every single repetition is going  to be different not only because of the  unusual shape of the bag  but also because of the shifting center  of gravity within the bag  it's also why trail running is so  valuable and wrestling with a human  opponent  what we're doing is creating robust  mental models so the movement is  already rehearsed in countless different  variations  i'd also like to take this further by  increasing the amount of variation  between and within  sets i talked about hybrid movements in  the 2020 batman training program  a movement like a renegade row is  extremely valuable because it involves  not only push-ups and rows  but also the transition between those  two movements the same goes for  something like a push-up into a hindu  push-up  into a burpee training out of sequence  like this also helps retention and  learning of new skills  this has been shown to be the case for  those practicing sports skills  here it's much more useful to train a  pass a dribble in a shot  rather than simply practicing shots or  passes for a defined chunk of time  this is called contextual interference  in contrast with chunking which is how  we typically train  by repeating a single movement over and  over and it makes sense  in the real world we don't chunk our  behaviors we need to be able to move  seamlessly from one to the other  completing a sequence of movements this  way also helps to keep us engaged as the  mind can't simply wonder  each movement must be crisp and sharp  and there must be no hesitation flowing  from one movement to the next  in an ideal world you'd respond to call  outs and attempt to transition quickly  as possible from one position to the  next  improving reaction times in a  multi-sensory three-dimensional space  this now becomes brain training as well  as physical training and more  importantly  it merges those two concepts remember in  my recent video on cognitive training we  discussed the importance of training  things like working memory  and likely other mental faculties in a  more 3d immersed environment  again this is where the sensory  integration comes into play which is how  the brain is  designed to operate and it's a world  apart from simply sitting in front of a  screen  and performing a dual n-back test it's  about viewing the entire system as one  cohesive whole  being able to use your physicality in  conjunction with rapid decision making  and awareness this has massive benefit  for athletes and of course  for batman again this should go at the  end of the workout things will be  rather cardio intensive and they won't  be the same focus on heavy repetitions  or complex movements  to stimulate hypertrophy and strength  gains  so these are the concepts behind the  2021 batman training program  that i'm dubbing the i am vengeance  workout if you want to see the full  version then head over to the website  where everything is listed in terms of  sets and repetitions  and split into a set number of days  these last two videos have been rather  big projects but i've really  appreciated all the support and the  fantastic response that these have  gotten  anyways next week i'm going to be  turning things down a little bit going a  bit back to normal  and among other things i'm going to be  discussing a functional training program  for complete beginners that means those  of us who can't even do a single push-up  i hope you found this one useful and  interesting guys a surprising number of  people have been following the  batman trading program from 2020 so i  hope that this will be  equally successful of course if you want  an even more in-depth training program  that's designed for  everybody then you can check out my  ebook and training program  super functional training that includes  a periodized  full program that focuses not only on  strength and  losing weight but also on mobility on  brain training  on explosiveness on on proprioception  and all the rest there's a discount on  that right now whilst we're all  struggling with the pandemic  alternatively you could pre-order my  upcoming book  functional training and beyond which is  a in-depth discussion  on the notion of functional training  it's available on amazon and many other  retailers you'll find links to that in  description down below  it comes out later this month in the us  and a little bit after that in  other countries and if you order it  right now there's a discount on  so you can benefit from that too thanks  again so much for watching these guys  i really appreciate it hope your 2021 is  off to a great start  and i'll see you next time thanks a ton  and bye for now  oh

